    </chooser>
    <slider x="10" step="1" y="525" max="100" width="195" display="density" height="50" min="0" direction="Horizontal" default="25.0" variable="density" units="%"></slider>
    <note x="10" y="580" backgroundDark="0" fontSize="12" width="195" markdown="false" height="20" textColorDark="-1" textColorLight="-16777216" backgroundLight="0">4. RUN</note>
    <button x="10" y="645" height="35" disableUntilTicks="true" forever="true" kind="Observer" width="96" display="paint cells">paint-cells</button>
    <button x="110" y="645" height="35" disableUntilTicks="true" forever="false" kind="Observer" width="96" display="clear row">clear-row</button>
    <switch x="10" y="685" height="40" on="false" variable="auto-continue?" width="195" display="auto-continue?"></switch>
    <note x="215" y="680" backgroundDark="0" fontSize="11" width="470" markdown="false" height="20" textColorDark="-1" textColorLight="-16777216" backgroundLight="0">Top row, used when INITIAL-CONDITION is "from text". It is centered in the row, so it need not fill the whole width.</note>
    <input x="215" multiline="true" y="705" height="70" variable="input-initial-condition" type="string" width="470">[0 1 0 1 1 0 0 1 0]</input>
    <button x="10" y="605" height="35" disableUntilTicks="false" forever="false" kind="Observer" width="95">setup</button>
    <button x="110" y="605" height="35" disableUntilTicks="true" forever="true" kind="Observer" width="95">go</button>
    <note x="215" y="585" backgroundDark="0" fontSize="11" width="470" markdown="false" height="20" textColorDark="-1" textColorLight="-16777216" backgroundLight="0">Rule table in Wolfram order (last entry is the all-0 neighborhood). Any of these work: [0 0 0 1 1 1 1 0]   00011110   #30. Presets, LOAD RULE and the rule table fill this in for you.</note>
    <input x="215" multiline="true" y="605" height="70" variable="input-rule" type="string" width="470">[0 0 0 1 0 1 0 0 2 2 2 0 1 2 2 1 1 0 0 2 0 2 2 1 1 2 0]</input>
    <monitor x="690" precision="17" y="585" height="60" fontSize="10" width="120" display="rule size">rule-size</monitor>
    <monitor x="690" precision="17" y="650" height="60" fontSize="11" width="120" display="# of possible rules">(word num-states " ^ " rule-size)</monitor>
    <button x="690" y="715" height="35" disableUntilTicks="true" forever="false" kind="Observer" width="120" display="copy row to box">copy-row-to-text</button>
    <button x="10" y="395" height="35" disableUntilTicks="false" forever="false" kind="Observer" width="96" display="save rule">save-rule</button>
    <button x="110" y="395" height="35" disableUntilTicks="false" forever="false" kind="Observer" width="96" display="load rule">load-rule</button>
  </widgets>
  <info><![CDATA[
## WHAT IS IT?

This is a model of one-dimensional cellular automata. A 1D cellular automaton is a row of cells, each holding one of a small number of states, which all update together in step: every cell looks at itself and its nearest neighbors, and a rule says what it becomes next. Each new row is drawn below the one that produced it, so the picture on screen is a history, with time running downwards.

Elementary 1D cellular automata models have two states and a neighborhood of three cells, which gives the 256 "elementary" rules that Stephen Wolfram catalogued. This model generalizes that. The number of states and the size of the neighborhood are both adjustable, so the same machinery covers the elementary rules, the three-color rules, and much larger families with arbitrary number of states and neighbors. Widening either one enlarges the space of possible rules very quickly.

Cellular automata are studied because extremely simple local rules can produce behavior that looks incredibly complex. Some rules settle into blank or striped uniformity, some repeat forever, some produce nested triangular patterns, and a few produce output that passes statistical tests for randomness despite being completely determined.


## HOW IT WORKS

Every cell holds a state, written as a whole number from 0 up to one less than the number of states. State 0 is shown in black; the others each get their own color. In the two-state case the cells are simply black and white.

To work out what a cell becomes, the model looks at a block of cells centered above it. The radius says how far that block reaches to either side, so a radius of 1 reads three cells, a radius of 2 reads five, and in general the neighborhood is twice the radius plus one cell wide. The leftmost and rightmost cells of a row are treated as neighbors of each other, so the row wraps around like a ring.

The rule is a lookup table. It has one entry for every neighborhood that could possibly occur, and that entry gives the state the cell below becomes. With two states and radius 1 there are eight possible neighborhoods, so the table has eight entries. With three states and radius 2 there are 243. In general the table has as many entries as the number of states raised to the power of the neighborhood width, which is what the `rule size` monitor reports.

### How a rule is represented

The entries are listed in a fixed order, so a bare list of numbers is enough to specify the whole rule. The order counts the neighborhoods downwards, starting from the one where every cell is in the highest state and ending with the one where every cell is 0. For two states and radius 1, that reads:

    neighborhood    111   110   101   100   011   010   001   000
    becomes          0     0     0     1     1     1     1     0

That table is Rule 30. Written as a list it is [0 0 0 1 1 1 1 0], and because every state is a single digit it can equally be written 00011110. The model accepts either form, and also tolerates commas.

To see which entry applies to a given neighborhood, read the neighborhood itself as a numeral in base "number of states", most significant digit on the left. The neighborhood 100 is the numeral 4, and the entries are listed from highest numeral down to 0, so 100 is matched by the fifth entry from the right, which is 1. The cell below a 1-0-0 neighborhood therefore turns on.

### Rule numbers

Reading the whole list of outputs as a single numeral, again in base "number of states", gives the rule a number. The outputs above are 00011110, which in base 2 is 30, which is why this automaton is called Rule 30. The same arithmetic works for any number of states: with three states the outputs are digits in base 3, and the rule number counts through all the three-state rules.

The rule number is a compact name for a rule, but it only stays usable while the rules can be counted exactly. There are as many rules as the number of states raised to the power of the table size, which the `# of possible rules` monitor shows. With two states and radius 1 that is 2^8, a comfortable 256. With four states and radius 1 it is 4^64, a number far too large to hold exactly, and the rule number monitor reports "too large". Rules that big are still perfectly usable; they simply have to be handled as tables rather than as numbers.

### The model run

The top row is the initial condition. Every row after it is computed from the row above by applying the rule to each cell in parallel. When the bottom of the screen is reached the run stops, or continues from the top using the last row as its new starting point.


## HOW TO USE IT

The interface is arranged in four numbered groups, and works top to bottom.

### 1. World and states

`num-states` is how many states a cell can hold. Set to 2 you get the familiar black-and-white automata; higher values add more colors.

`radius` is how far a cell looks to either side, which determines the neighborhood of each cell. The `radius` and `num-states` determines the size of the rule table, shown in the `rule size` monitor, and the number of possible rules, shown next to it.

`grid size` sets how wide the world is, and therefore how many cells a row holds and how many rows fit on screen. A larger world shows finer detail but draws smaller cells.

### 2. Rule

`rule-source` decides where the rule comes from, and it is applied on `setup`. The rule doesn't change after `setup` is pressed.

- random builds a fresh random rule every time you press `setup`.
- from rule box reads whatever is written in the rule box below the view.
- the remaining choices are named example rules. These set `num-states` and `radius` to match, since a rule means nothing without them, and they fill the rule box in so you can see and edit what they contain.

The rule box takes a table in either form, [0 0 0 1 1 1 1 0] or 00011110, or a rule number written with a leading hash, such as #110. The hash form is the quickest way to call up a rule you know by name, and it only works while the rule number is exact.

The `rule number` monitor names the rule currently loaded, or reports "too large" when there are too many rules to count exactly.

`edit rule table` draws the rule in the view: one small diagram per neighborhood, showing the cells that get read with the state they produce underneath. Click that lower block to step it through the states, and the rule box and rule number follow along as you click. The view is given over to the table while this is running, so press `setup` when you are done to go back to the automaton. If the table needs more room than `grid-size` allows, the world is enlarged to fit and `setup` puts it back. Very large tables cannot be drawn usefully and are refused.

`save rule` and `load rule` write and read a rule as a small text file. The number of states and the radius travel with the rule, so loading one sets those for you and puts the rule in the box ready to run.

### 3. Initial condition

`initial-condition` chooses the top row: a single cell in the highest state at the center, a random row, a blank row to draw on yourself, or the contents of the initial condition box below the view.

`density` is the percentage of cells that start in a non-zero state when the random setting is used.

The initial condition box takes a list of states. It is centered in the row and padded with zeros, so it does not have to be as wide as the world.

### 4. Run

`setup` builds the rule, sizes the world, and lays down the top row.

`go` runs the automaton. Pressed again after reaching the bottom, it continues from the top using the last row.

`paint cells` lets you draw the top row by hand: click a cell to step it to the next state, or drag to smear that state along the row. `clear row` empties it again. Painting also works partway through a run, so you can disturb an automaton while it is going.

`auto-continue?` makes the run carry on from the top automatically instead of stopping at the bottom.

`copy row to box` writes the row currently being computed into the initial condition box, so a pattern you have painted or arrived at can be kept and used again.


## THINGS TO NOTICE

Start with Rule 30 from a single cell. The left side of the pattern is regular and striped while the right side looks disordered, all from one rule and one starting cell.

Rule 90 from a single cell draws a Sierpinski triangle. Nothing in the rule mentions triangles, and nothing tells the cells how large the pattern should be.

Compare a single starting cell with a random starting row for the same rule. Some rules produce much the same texture either way; others depend entirely on how they began.

Watch how quickly the number of possible rules grows. Two states and radius 1 gives 256 of them. Adding one state, or one cell of radius, makes the count astronomically larger, which is why it is difficult to explore all the possible rules when the the number of states and radius are large.

Some rules erase themselves within a few rows, ending in a blank or uniformly colored screen. Interesting behavior is rarer than uninteresting behavior, and rarer still as the number of states grows.

The two edges of the world are joined, so a pattern that runs off one side reappears on the other. Wide patterns eventually collide with themselves.


## THINGS TO TRY

Type #110 into the rule box with two states and radius 1, and run it from a random row. Rule 110 is known to be capable of universal computation.

Work through a handful of the 256 elementary rules by number and sort them by what they do: dying out, repeating, nesting, or looking random. Rules 0, 4, 90, 30 and 110 are useful landmarks.

Set the number of states to 3 and pick random rules repeatedly. How many do you have to try before finding one that neither dies nor fills the screen with noise?

Open `edit rule table` on Rule 30, change a single entry, and run it. Some entries barely matter and others change the picture completely. Which are which, and can you predict it before running?

Draw a top row by hand with `paint cells`: two cells a little apart, then run a rule and watch what happens where the two patterns meet.

Save a rule you like, change the sliders around, then load it back. The saved file carries the number of states and radius with it.

Increase the radius to 2 with two states and look for rules that behave differently from anything reachable with radius 1.

Use the random initial condition and vary `density`. Does a rule that looks chaotic from a dense start behave the same way from a sparse one?


## EXTENDING THE MODEL

Add rule families. A totalistic rule makes the outcome depend only on the sum of the neighborhood rather than on which cell holds which state, so it needs far fewer numbers to describe: seven instead of twenty-seven for three states and radius 1. That is a small enough set to explore exhaustively, and it would also make the rule table drawable for much larger neighborhoods, since it would need one diagram per sum rather than one per neighborhood.

Add a way to mutate the rule currently running, changing a few entries at a time. Interesting rules tend to have interesting neighbors, so nudging a good rule is a far better search than drawing new ones at random.

Add Langton's lambda parameter: build random rules in which a chosen fraction of the neighborhoods produce a non-zero state. Sweeping that fraction upwards takes the behavior from frozen, through repeating and complex, to chaotic.

Let the initial condition be drawn at any world size by magnifying it, in the same way the rule table draws its cells as blocks rather than single cells.

Classical cellular automata run on an infinite row, while this one joins the two ends into a ring. How would you get closer to an infinite row, and how would you tell the difference?

Let the rule change as the automaton runs, or let different parts of the row follow different rules.

Make the automaton two-dimensional, so that each cell reads the cells around it in a plane rather than along a line.


## NETLOGO FEATURES

The rule table is drawn in the ordinary view rather than in a separate window. Each cell of each diagram is a block of patches rather than a single patch, which is what makes the diagrams large enough to click on. Mouse handling uses `mouse-down?` together with `mouse-xcor` and `mouse-ycor`, comparing against the previous state of the mouse so that one click counts once and a drag is told apart from a click.

Because the rule table sometimes needs more room than the automaton does, the world is resized while it is on screen and restored on the next `setup`. This is also why several buttons refuse to act while the table is showing: the world on screen is not the automaton at that moment.

`setup` deliberately avoids `clear-all`, which would reset the rule it has just built along with everything else, and clears only what needs clearing.

A saved rule is written as a single list, so reading it back takes one call to `file-read` with no parsing. Since opening a file for writing appends to it, the model deletes any existing file first, which otherwise produces a doubled and unreadable file on the second save.


## RELATED MODELS

CA 1D Elementary explores the 256 two-state, radius 1 rules, with switches for the eight cases instead of a table.

CA 1D Totalistic explores the 2,187 three-state totalistic rules, where the outcome depends on the sum of the neighborhood.

CA 1D Rule 30, CA 1D Rule 90, CA 1D Rule 110 and CA 1D Rule 250 each show a single well-known rule.

CA Stochastic applies rules with a probability rather than with certainty.

CA Continuous allows cell states to vary continuously instead of in discrete steps.

Life is a two-dimensional cellular automaton.


## CREDITS AND REFERENCES

The first cellular automaton was conceived by John von Neumann in the late 1940s, following a suggestion by Stanislaw Ulam, and was completed and documented by Arthur Burks. John Conway's Game of Life brought two-dimensional automata to a wide audience in the 1970s. Chris Langton, Tom Toffoli and Stephen Wolfram carried out much of the foundational work on classification in the following decade, and Wolfram's numbering of the 256 elementary rules is the scheme this model generalizes.

Langton, C. 1984. Self-reproduction in cellular automata. Physica D 10, 134-144.

Langton, C. 1990. Computation at the edge of chaos: phase transitions and emergent computation. Physica D 42, 12-37.

Wolfram, S. 1986. Theory and Applications of Cellular Automata. World Scientific.

Wolfram, S. 2002. A New Kind of Science. Wolfram Media, Champaign, IL.

Cook, M. 2004. Universality in elementary cellular automata. Complex Systems 15, 1-40.

## HOW TO CITE

If you mention this model or the NetLogo software in a publication, we ask that you include the citations below.

For the model itself:

* Gurkan, C. and Wilensky, U. (2026).  NetLogo CA 1D General model. [link].  Center for Connected Learning and Computer-Based Modeling, Northwestern University, Evanston, IL.

Please cite the NetLogo software as:

* Wilensky, U. (1999). NetLogo. http://ccl.northwestern.edu/netlogo/. Center for Connected Learning and Computer-Based Modeling, Northwestern University, Evanston, IL.

]]></info>
